{-
   **A `@bits(n)` destination is reached by converting to the natural width and then narrowing**, and
   a `@bits(n)` value reaches everything its declared range reaches.

   # resolver: ownership

   [repr.md](doc/spec/repr.md#bit-width-refinements-bits) states the pair a refinement is made of - a
   load widens, a store narrows - and both halves are about a single position. What neither half
   answers on its own is what happens when the two types on either side of a conversion are *not* a
   refinement and the thing it refines, and that is the whole of what this fixture is about.

   The load's widening is the **machine's** and not the type's: a read of a refined place is a value
   of that refinement, and what puts it at a natural width is the conversion each use performs. That
   is what `asSize` rests on, and it is why the arithmetic in `pick` still happens at `U32`.

   Written for one stage because the shipping resolver refuses `pick`: it compares the two arms of an
   `if` for identity and a refinement is not identical to what it refines, so an arm at `@bits(4) U32`
   beside one at `U32` is "branches of this expression have different types" there. The rest compiles
   under both, and lives here rather than beside them so that the four claims stay together.

   The four:

   - `fromByte` supplies a `U8` where twenty bits are wanted. Neither the ladder nor a refinement
     relates the pair on its own - `U8` widens to `U32` and `U32` narrows to the refinement - so it
     is the composition or it is nothing. It used to be nothing, and the way it failed is worth
     recording: the widening was selected (an instance written for `U64` is reachable from every
     refinement of it, which is the point), it produced a value at `U32`, and the mismatch surfaced
     as an *internal* error about a return that hands back the wrong type.
   - `spread` goes the other way, from a refinement into a wider one of a different canonical type.
   - `pick` is the unification: two positions that have to end up as one type, with nothing outside
     them to say which. The answer is the natural width, because a refinement decides no type.
   - `asSize` is a refinement reaching a **target-width** type, and is the case the rule is for:
     `Size` guarantees thirty-one signed bits, so twenty unsigned ones fit and a whole `U32` does
     not. `lib/Core/String.yana` stores its length as a `@bits(30) U32` and hands it back as a
     `Size`, which is where this was found.

   Every case is a distinct power of two, so `main` is 31 when they all pass.
-}

data Box {small: @bits(4) U32, wide: @bits(20) U32}

-- Two steps: `U8` widens to `U32`, and the store narrows that to twenty bits. Neither is the whole
-- conversion and the second cannot be written as a rung, since no rung names a refinement.
fn fromByte(x: U8) -> @bits(20) U32 = x

-- A refinement's *declared* range is what it reaches with, which is more than its canonical type
-- reaches: twenty unsigned bits fit forty, where a whole `U32` would not fit a `@bits(20) U64`.
fn spread(b: Box) -> @bits(40) U64 = b.wide

-- The arms unify to the canonical width, because a refinement decides no type. The ascription is
-- what keeps `b.small` at its own type through the merge rather than converting it in the arm.
fn pick(c: Bool, b: Box) -> U32 = if c: (b.small :: @bits(4) U32) else 7

-- A refinement reaches what its *declared range* reaches, and a target-width destination is the
-- case that matters: `Size` guarantees thirty-one signed bits, which twenty unsigned ones fit. The
-- same function taking a plain `U32` is refused, and correctly.
fn asSize(b: Box) -> Size = b.wide

-- The narrowing is real and not just a type: 300 does not fit four bits, and 300 & 15 is 12.
fn masked(x: U32) -> @bits(4) U32 = x

fn check(got: U32, want: U32, bit: Int) -> Int = if got == want then bit else 0

fn main() -> Int:
    let b = Box {small: 3, wide: 1000}

    return check(fromByte(200), 200, 1) +
        (if spread(b) == 1000 then 2 else 0) +
        check(pick(True, b) + pick(False, b), 10, 4) +
        check(masked(300), 12, 8) +
        (if asSize(b) == 1000 then 16 else 0)
